1. Executive decision
Build Chessbitz as a static-site-generated (SSG) chess puzzle platform. Puzzle moves, board state, progressive hints, local progress, sharing, and worksheet generation should run in the browser. GitHub Actions (GHA) should curate and validate data, run tests, create a pre-generated date-to-puzzle manifest, and deploy the static site. A daily puzzle must not depend on a successful scheduled workflow at the moment of the daily reset.

The revised product remains no-login and subscription-free, but treats legal provenance, privacy, advertising behaviour, child safety, software licensing, security, and takedown handling as release requirements rather than optional documentation.
1.1 Decisions
- Puzzle source: use the specific Lichess open puzzle dataset stated as CC0, and preserve downloaded source evidence, checksum, retrieval date, schema version, and per-puzzle provenance. The Lichess database page states that the exports may be used commercially, modified, and redistributed. Do not scrape commercial chess sites or assume all Lichess datasets/assets share the puzzle dataset's licence. [R1][R15]
- Colour-swapped variants: may be generated as a product feature only through a mathematically defined board symmetry transform and full legal-move validation. This is not a way to “escape compliance”; CC0 already permits modification and redistribution of that specific dataset. Never falsify, delete or obscure source provenance. [R1][R16]
- Browser rules: use chess.js for move legality and state management, after pinning and recording the exact version/licence. [R2]
- Board UI: prefer a version of react-chessboard whose exact package version and MIT licence are verified at build time. Do not adopt Chessground unless Chessbitz deliberately accepts GPL obligations, including making the covered combined work/source available as required by the licence. [R3][R4]
- Engine: do not ship Stockfish in the MVP. If added later, make a separate GPLv3 distribution-compliance decision, including required licence and corresponding-source availability for the exact distributed build. [R5]
- Advertising: Monetag is a third-party ad provider, not a component of the chess engine. Its scripts must not block the board, hide controls, redirect unexpectedly, or trigger monetisation on core puzzle clicks. Launch with a conservative format approved in the publisher dashboard and verify every format under the publisher agreement. [R6]
- Privacy: no account or upload does not mean no data processing. Hosting/CDN logs and advertising tags can process IP addresses, browser/device data, identifiers, and interaction data. Maintain a vendor/data-flow inventory and gate non-essential tracking using a consent mechanism where required. [R7][R8]
- Children: design for general audiences and safe use. Do not profile children or intentionally serve behavioural advertising to child users. Because chess worksheets may attract minors, obtain legal/ad-network confirmation before promoting Chessbitz specifically to children or schools. [R9]
- Brand: “Chessbitz” is not cleared by this document. Complete an Indian trademark and common-law search, including phonetic variants and related software/education/advertising classes, before spending materially on branding. [R10]
