6. Visual design system and user experience
6.1 Design intent
Chessbitz should look like a polished, editorial chess-learning product rather than a casino, ad-network landing page, or full-game client. The default brand identity is Midnight Indigo with blue-gray and warm-ivory chessboard squares; green is not the brand colour. The board and tactical task are the visual priority. Use familiar Staunton piece silhouettes, concise instructions, predictable controls, and clear post-move feedback. Indian and international users should see the same core visual system; language is not encoded only by colour or icon.

Design principles:
- Board-first layout; ads never overlay the board, move targets, hints, reset, solution playback, or print/share controls.
- Familiar chess conventions; no novelty pieces, emojis, or abstract icons in place of chessmen.
- Low cognitive load: one primary action per screen, progressive hints, and restrained motion.
- Mobile-first: the entire 8x8 board remains visible without horizontal scrolling.
- Text and icons supplement colour. Correct/incorrect states must never be distinguished by colour alone.
- Default to a light theme; an optional dark UI theme may change the page shell, but the chessboard stays on the approved accessible board palette unless the user chooses another tested board theme.
6.2 Brand and colour tokens
The default palette is Midnight Indigo + Blue-Gray + Warm Ivory. Do not use green as the main brand, primary-button, or default-board colour. Green may appear only as a semantic colour within a selected board theme or a tested success state. Do not let a coding agent invent colours without updating the token table and reviewing contrast.
