6.3 Board and piece specification
Board geometry
- Use an exact 8-column by 8-row square grid, aspect-ratio: 1 / 1, with all squares equal. Desktop board maximum width: 640 CSS px. Mobile board width: min(100%, 640px) within a container with 16px minimum side gutters. No horizontal board scrolling.
- Desktop puzzle layout: board on the left, puzzle task/hints/move status in a right-hand panel. On screens below approximately 760 CSS px, stack the board first and controls directly beneath it.
- The player whose turn it is to solve the puzzle is at the bottom by default. Display a visible rotate-board control. Coordinate labels are enabled by default and must remain readable on both square colours.
- Use the same board geometry for the playable puzzle, solution playback, embedded puzzle and print/PDF renderer, with layout changes only where necessary for paper.
Pieces
- Use a conventional Staunton silhouette for all 12 piece types. Piece assets must be local, deterministic SVG or another documented, compatible format; do not use emoji, Unicode chess glyphs as the default rendering, competitor screenshots, or copied proprietary pieces.
- Preferred default white pieces: white fill #FFFFFF, deep-indigo outline #252B52, and limited internal detail. Preferred default black pieces: deep-indigo fill #252B52, white outline/detail #FFFFFF. Use enough stroke and silhouette separation for both pieces to remain identifiable on both square colours and every selectable board theme.
- Maintain an asset register for all 12 piece SVGs: author/source, licence, exact file path, checksum, and any modifications. Prefer original project-created SVGs or assets with explicit permission and compatible licence.
- Do not depend on remote image/font hosts for piece rendering. If a selected chessboard component bundles piece art, inspect the exact installed package and bundled-asset licences separately.
Board annotations and state
- Legal destination indicators: restrained indigo dots/rings with sufficient contrast; do not use the same treatment as the focus ring.
- Selected source square: distinct focus outline, not a full-board colour change.
- Last move: translucent amber overlay plus from/to indication.
- Check/checkmate: king-square outline plus readable status text and screen-reader announcement.
- Wrong move: concise message, error icon and optional move reset; never use only a red square.
- Correct move: success icon/text and a subtle animation; honour prefers-reduced-motion.
- Hints: reveal information progressively (piece, destination area, then exact move), but only from the validated solution line. Label the current hint level and explain that a hint was used for the local score/streak rule.
- Include undo/reset, show solution, and move-list playback as distinct controls. No Monetag click handler may attach to any board or puzzle control.
