7. Architecture and deployment
7.1 Static-first architecture
1. Source stage: download the puzzle data from the documented CC0 puzzle dataset location; capture retrieval date, original filename, checksum, schema and relevant licence snapshot.
2. Curation stage: deduplicate; normalise metadata; validate FEN; apply the setup move correctly; verify full solution legality and completion; detect empty/ambiguous lines; record excluded rows.
3. Manifest stage: choose a reproducible, deterministic mapping of dates to puzzle IDs for a rolling horizon (recommended 12 months). Keep the timezone explicit (UTC by default, or choose Asia/Kolkata before launch and use it consistently everywhere).
4. Build stage: build static landing/category/detail pages, JSON shards, sitemap, canonical URLs, robots rules and social preview metadata.
5. QA stage: unit tests, puzzle-line replay, link validation, policy route checks, dependency/licence scanning, privacy/tag scan and browser E2E tests.
6. Deploy stage: least-privilege GitHub Actions deploy to the selected static host; retain a deploy rollback path.
7. Maintenance stage: scheduled workflow extends the daily manifest and updates source data; if it fails, the previously deployed manifest remains valid.
7.2 Core data and privacy boundary
Puzzle metadata is static public content. Board moves and hints run locally. Progress/preference data stays in the browser unless the user explicitly exports it. No move history, puzzle progress, student name, or worksheet metadata is uploaded by Chessbitz in the MVP. Hosting/security logs and third-party advertising requests are separate flows and must be explained honestly.
7.3 Puzzle source processing rules
- Use the puzzle dataset specifically documented as CC0; do not infer the licence of all game broadcasts, studies, pieces, themes, software or media from the dataset licence.
- Apply the first Moves token to the source FEN before presenting the puzzle; that first token is the opponent/setup move, and the next token begins the solver's line. Add fixtures proving this behaviour. [R1]
- Any generated color-symmetric variant must follow the transform specification in Section 9.4, retain complete provenance, and pass the same position/move validation suite as the source puzzle.
- Store source_dataset, source_record_id, source_downloaded_at, source_sha256, source_license, schema_version, normalization_version, validated_at, validator_version, reason_for_exclusion, and the transformations performed.
- Validate every stored move through the chess rules library. Keep both the original record and normalised output in a restricted build artifact or source archive according to retention policy.
- Include a static denylist/withdrawal file that suppresses disputed or defective puzzle IDs at build time. It must take precedence over every catalogue and daily manifest.
- Do not copy explanatory prose, graphics, puzzle diagrams, article text, player photos, videos or proprietary site UI from Chess.com or other publishers. Write original teaching explanations.
- Keep raw corpus files outside the public build output. Do not deploy a multi-million-row raw file or expose future schedule details unnecessarily.
8. Intellectual property and licensing controls
8.1 Puzzle and data rights
The Lichess open database site states that its database exports are released under CC0 and may be modified and redistributed commercially. This is a suitable primary source for the initial puzzle corpus if the exact puzzle export and its current licence are confirmed at ingestion time. [R1] Store the evidence. A licence to a dataset is not a blanket licence to copy the provider's software, logo, website design, brand, illustrations, other datasets or every item linked from it.

Do not scrape commercial puzzle services, republish book exercises or copy puzzle text/diagrams from copyrighted books, videos, apps or websites without documented permission. Copyright treatment of a bare chess position or move sequence may differ by jurisdiction and facts; do not rely on an unreviewed theory that all chess puzzles are uncopyrightable. Use a documented, licensed source and obtain local counsel review if a rightsholder disputes an item.
