8.4 Colour-swapped and mirrored puzzle variants: rules and compliance
This is a product transformation, not a compliance workaround. Do not describe or implement it as a way to conceal the source. The Lichess open database page states that the puzzle export is CC0 and that users may modify and redistribute it, including for commercial use. That explicit permission is the relevant starting point for the dataset. Retain evidence of the exact dataset licence and download. CC0 does not grant rights to Lichess trademarks, unrelated software, other exports with different terms, or third-party artwork. [R1][R15][R16]

Correct the product language: the puzzle CSV does not contain a general field saying “White wins in N moves.” It contains fields such as PuzzleId, FEN, Moves, Rating, Themes, GameUrl and related metadata. Themes can include mate-length tags; the actual position and validated move line determine the task. The source FEN is before the opponent/setup move, so the first move in Moves must be applied before showing the player the puzzle; the next move begins the solver's line. Never change a label from White to Black without transforming and validating the actual position and moves. [R1]

Approved transformation (standard chess only): create a mathematically symmetric color-swapped/rank-reflected position, not merely pieces recoloured on the same squares.
1. Normalize the source record, apply the setup move, and validate the solver position and remaining solution line.
2. Reflect every square vertically: files remain the same and each rank r maps to 9 - r (for example, e2 maps to e7). Swap the colour of every piece at the same time. This allows white pawn movement to correspond to black pawn movement.
3. Flip the active side (w to b, b to w). Swap castling-right case (K to k, Q to q, and conversely), preserving kingside/queenside. Reflect the en-passant target square's rank if present. Normalize FEN castling-right order and counters according to the chess library's accepted FEN representation.
4. Apply the same square transform to every UCI move in the solution: transform the from-square and to-square; retain promotion piece type. Replay the transformed line from the transformed position using a rules library. This must cover castling, en passant, promotion/underpromotion and terminal states. Exclude a record if any transformed move is illegal or the transformed line/objective is inconsistent.
5. Generate a distinct Chessbitz variant ID and record transformed_from, source dataset and source ID, source licence, retrieval date, source checksum, transform version, validation version, and resulting FEN/moves. Never replace or discard the original source record from the internal provenance registry.
6. Recheck metadata: recompute or label the difficulty/rating as an estimate rather than assuming the source user rating remains valid for the new Chessbitz variant; check tactical-theme labels, objective text and all solution/hint outputs. For mate puzzles, verify the transformed mate sequence and exact mate length through legal replay; any stronger uniqueness claim requires separate validation.
7. Test round-trip symmetry where appropriate: transform a source puzzle twice and confirm it returns to the normalized source position/move line, subject to FEN normalization. Keep fixtures for every special-move class.
Do not do these things: simply swap piece colours in-place; alter “White” to “Black” in a title while retaining the old position; delete source fields in order to make origins harder to identify; label a transformed line as original analysis authored by Chessbitz; or claim that transformation itself makes content legally safer. For the CC0 puzzle data, modification is already permitted by the documented dataset licence; provenance, correctness and other rights still require appropriate controls.

Public disclosure: on the /licenses page, it is good practice to say that Chessbitz uses the Lichess open puzzle database (CC0) and may normalize or symmetry-transform positions. CC0 does not itself make attribution a condition, but transparent source acknowledgement is recommended. Do not use Lichess logos or imply affiliation. [R16]
9. Data protection, cookies and third-party advertising
9.1 Data inventory
Data/source	Default treatment	Required notice/control
Puzzle FEN, moves, tags, rating metadata	Public licensed content	Provenance and correction process
Local theme, board orientation, solved state, streak	Browser-only local storage	Explain purpose; give reset and export options; avoid transmitting by default
Web host/CDN request logs	Vendor-controlled technical records may include IP, user agent, time and requested URL	Verify host, region, retention, access and subprocessors; explain in privacy policy
Monetag/ad-tech requests	May process IP, browser/device identifiers, ad events and other signals; actual fields depend on implementation	Review current vendor terms/privacy documentation; use required consent signals; list vendor in policy
Contact messages, if implemented	Name/email/message voluntarily supplied	Keep optional, minimise content, restrict access and publish a retention rule
Analytics, if later added	Disabled until approved	Consent by region where required; event minimisation; no puzzle-history upload unless disclosed
Student name/date on worksheet	Browser-generated; must not be sent to servers	State that it is printed locally; do not collect through forms


Do not state “we collect no personal data” unless verified against actual hosting, CDN, advertising and security telemetry. A site can process personal data even when it has no login system.
9.2 India - DPDP and other applicable law
The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025 have phased commencement. The Rules were notified on 13 November 2025; Rules 1, 2 and 17-21 commenced on publication, Rule 4 after one year, and Rules 3, 5-16, 22-23 after 18 months. The associated Act provisions are also phased. The main operational notice/consent and data-fiduciary framework is expected to become central around May 2027; verify exact effective dates, corrigenda and any new notifications with Indian counsel before launch and again before those provisions apply. [R7][R8]

Build to the intended standard now: clearly identify the operator, describe data categories and purposes, separate required from optional processing, provide rights/complaint contact, document processors and transfers, minimise data, apply safeguards and define deletion/retention. Do not use the transitional period as a reason to ship a misleading or unreviewed privacy experience.
9.3 International privacy and cookie rules
Visitors in the EEA/UK and other jurisdictions may trigger privacy, ePrivacy/cookie, children’s or state privacy requirements. Location alone is not a sufficient legal analysis; assess the actual audience, targeting, personalisation and vendor relationships. For EEA/UK visits, do not load non-essential analytics/advertising storage or tracking before valid consent where required. Accept all, Reject all and Settings must be similarly clear; consent must be revocable from a persistent “Privacy/Cookie settings” footer link. Do not treat a privacy policy alone as cookie consent. [R12]

Use a consent-management platform (CMP) compatible with the selected Monetag implementation and applicable frameworks. Before choosing a CMP, verify the consent signals supported by Monetag, whether the ad tags honour those signals, which vendors/partners execute, and which cookies/storage items are set. If this cannot be technically verified, do not serve behavioural/personalised ads in jurisdictions where consent is required; disable non-essential ad technology until it can be implemented correctly.
9.4 Cookie and local storage inventory
Before production, run a clean-browser scan on first visit, after Accept, Reject and Settings, and after withdrawal. Record all first- and third-party cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB entries, pixels, beacons, network requests, identifiers, purpose, vendor, duration, region, consent state and deletion behaviour. The public cookie policy must list actual names and lifetimes from this scan. Do not publish invented or assumed cookie names.

Local progress is not uploaded by the Chessbitz application. Provide controls to clear progress. If privacy law treats local storage or similar device access as consent-controlled in a visitor's jurisdiction, gate optional storage appropriately. Essential functionality should be limited to what the user requested or what is genuinely required to operate the requested service.
9.5 Monetag rules
- Re-read the live Monetag publisher agreement/content rules before approval and whenever the vendor changes terms. Monetag states publisher content must not infringe third-party rights or violate law. [R6]
- Do not generate artificial traffic, use bots, buy incentivised/invalid traffic, click your own ads, ask users to click ads, rotate tags to fabricate impressions, or place ads where a board interaction is mistaken for an ad click.
- Do not use pop-under/on-click formats on the chessboard, hint, reset, solution, print or share controls. Do not hide close controls or trigger redirects as a side effect of ordinary moves.
- Keep ad code separate from game state. Failure, blocking or delay of ad script must not prevent solving, hinting, printing or downloading a user-selected worksheet.
- Test ads on mobile and desktop, including keyboard and touch. Monitor complaints, deceptive/malicious creatives, redirects, CLS/LCP, bounce rate and revenue. Pause the affected format if it disrupts primary tasks or a harmful ad is detected.
- Do not assume Monetag's consent or privacy obligations are automatically solved by putting its name in the privacy policy.
10. Children and family use
Chessbitz may be used by minors even without accounts. US COPPA can apply to services directed to children under 13 or to services with actual knowledge of collecting personal information from them. India’s DPDP framework defines children as under 18 and includes special treatment for children's data and tracking/targeted advertising; the relevant provisions must be checked against their commencement date. [R7][R9]

For the MVP:
- Do not request date of birth, school, precise location, real name, photo, account identifier or other child-specific data.
- Do not add chat, public profile, uploads, direct messaging, classroom roster uploads or public comments.
- Do not build behavioural profiles from puzzle solving.
- Do not intentionally direct personalised ads to children. Confirm ad-network suitability for general-audience educational content and the ability to suppress prohibited targeting.
- If the marketing plan becomes child-directed or the ad provider cannot give adequate child-safe controls, pause child-directed promotion and get specialist advice before monetising such pages.
- Give parents/teachers a straightforward contact for privacy and content concerns; do not promise that third-party advertising is automatically child-safe.
11. Website policy pages and disclosures
Required routes, linked in the footer:
- /privacy - actual operator identity, data flows, purposes, vendors, rights, retention, international transfers and contact.
- /cookies - cookie/local-storage categories, actual inventory and consent settings.
- /terms - permitted use, IP, service availability and legally limited warranty/liability provisions.
- /disclaimer - educational nature, possible puzzle errors, no performance guarantee, third-party ads and links.
- /copyright - rights-holder notice/takedown/correction process; must be monitored.
- /licenses - source dataset declaration, third-party notices and OSS licence disclosures.
- /about - product purpose and clear non-affiliation statement.
- /security - responsible disclosure channel and supported report format.
- /accessibility - target standard, limitations and feedback channel.
- /contact - working contact details and response expectations.
All operator contact fields are mandatory pre-launch inputs, not optional copy. Do not publish policies that say a request may be sent to a placeholder or non-monitored address. Do not copy a generic template unchanged if it inaccurately describes actual scripts, data locations, the business entity or vendors.
12. Terms and disclaimer requirements
The companion Compliance Pack contains initial drafts. At minimum the live terms must address: acceptance/use restrictions; puzzle/education-only purpose; no chess rating, tournament or coaching certification; possible data/position/source errors; third-party ads and links; intellectual property and source notices; service change/availability; prohibited scraping/abuse; complaint/takedown channels; privacy/cookie documents; governing law and dispute language reviewed for operator status; and a limitation clause that does not purport to remove mandatory consumer/statutory rights.

A disclaimer is not a substitute for valid licences, transparent ad behaviour, a truthful privacy notice or security controls. Avoid absolute language such as “we have no liability under any circumstances” or “this website cannot be sued.” Such statements do not guarantee enforceability and can be misleading.
13. Security requirements and threat model
13.1 Threat model
Threat	Consequence	Required mitigation
Compromised GitHub token/action	Malicious production build or secret exposure	Least privilege, pin third-party actions by full commit SHA, protected branch/environment, secret scanning, MFA/passkeys
Malicious dependency or unreviewed licence	Supply-chain compromise or forced source disclosure	Lockfile, SBOM, licence allowlist, OSV/dependency alerts, review transitive and bundled assets
Poisoned or malformed puzzle row	Broken board, script error, misleading solution	Strict schema/FEN/line validation, bounded payloads, fuzz/fixture tests, denylist
Third-party ad script changes	Redirects, tracking, malicious creatives, broken UX	Tag isolation, consent checks, CSP monitoring, ad QA, pause switch, no ad code in move handlers
XSS through future content fields	Script execution or data theft	No unsanitised HTML, escape puzzle metadata, restrictive CSP, avoid user-generated HTML
Supply-chain/secret leak	Account takeover	No deployment secrets in public bundles or repo; rotate keys; GitHub OIDC where supported
Privacy over-collection	Regulatory complaint or loss of trust	Data minimisation, local-only progress, tag inventory, consent controls, retention/deletion tests
Copyright complaint	Takedown, cost, brand risk	Provenance, legal report mailbox, rapid review/suppression, preserve evidence, counsel escalation
Bad/fraudulent traffic	Monetag suspension or lost earnings	No paid bot traffic, no self-clicks, traffic-source monitoring, preserve evidence of legitimate referrers
