13.2 Secure build and hosting baseline
- Enforce MFA/passkeys on GitHub, hosting/CDN, domain registrar and Monetag accounts; use unique passwords and recovery codes in a secure password manager.
- Use protected main branch, pull-request review (even if the second reviewer is a documented self-review), required tests, pinned GitHub Actions by full commit SHA, minimal permissions, short-lived OIDC deployment credentials where supported, and protected deploy environments.
- Never put API keys, Monetag account credentials, private data or admin tokens in static bundles or public repository files. The static site has no secret backend.
- Enable dependency scanning, secret scanning, lockfile review and an SBOM; remove unmaintained or licence-unclear packages.
- Use HTTPS; add and test HSTS, X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, a restrictive Referrer-Policy, a narrow Permissions-Policy, and CSP including frame-ancestors. Adapt CSP to actual ad/CDN domains only after examining needed requests; do not use an unrestricted * policy as a quick fix.
- Use rel="noopener noreferrer" for external links opened in new tabs. Escape puzzle themes, titles and imported data as text; never inject upstream text as HTML.
- Scan production with clean browser profiles. Verify there are no unexpected cookies or outbound requests before consent in each relevant region.
- Provide /security.txt or an equivalent clear security contact and a safe reporting channel.
- Back up source/configuration, keep deploy logs and document rollback. Do not store sensitive user content because the product should not collect it.
13.3 CERT-In and incident response
CERT-In’s 28 April 2022 Directions include six-hour reporting for listed cyber incidents for in-scope entities and secure rolling 180-day retention of ICT logs within India. Whether and how a particular individual/business and its chosen static-hosting/logging model fall within each requirement must be confirmed; the operator must not assume static hosting automatically exempts it. [R13]

Before launch, ask counsel and the hosting provider: (a) is the operator in scope; (b) which logs are available; (c) where the logs are stored; (d) how to preserve/export them; (e) who is the designated incident point of contact; and (f) which incident classes and reporting timelines apply. If in scope, implement the applicable reporting and log-retention requirements.

Internal response sequence:
1. Triage immediately; preserve relevant logs/evidence and note UTC timestamps.
2. Disable compromised tags, revoke credentials, pause deployment or suppress affected content as needed.
3. Determine what systems/data were affected and whether a reportable cyber incident or personal-data breach occurred.
4. Follow the applicable CERT-In, DPDP, contract and foreign-law reporting clocks. Do not assume one notification satisfies every regime.
5. Correct or roll back, verify the fix, document decisions and follow up with affected people/regulators where required.
6. Review root cause, update controls and retain an incident record without keeping unnecessary personal data.
14. Copyright and content complaint workflow
Although the MVP has no user uploads, Chessbitz republishes a curated dataset and therefore needs a clear process for source disputes, copyright claims, trademark complaints, incorrect positions and defective solutions.

A report should request: reporter name and contact; exact URL and puzzle ID; work alleged to be infringed; basis/authority to act; source URL or evidence; requested action; good-faith statement and declaration of accuracy; electronic signature/name. Do not demand sensitive identity documents unless necessary and lawful. A legal contact reviews promptly, may temporarily suppress the puzzle through the denylist, preserves necessary evidence, contacts counsel for disputed/complex cases, and records action/reason/date. Provide a counter-notice/response path without promising a specific statutory safe harbour. Restore only after review and where appropriate. Repeatedly disputed content stays excluded pending resolution.
15. Static content quality and puzzle correctness
Every puzzle must pass: schema validation; valid FEN parse; setup-move application fixture; all source move tokens legal; side-to-move and expected objective match the UI label; full solution replay; terminal condition verified for mate puzzles; no invalid promotion/castling/en-passant state; duplicate check; move list size cap; no malformed strings/HTML; and regression tests.

Hints must derive from validated solution metadata and not invent unsupported variations. If wrong-move explanations are added, precompute and test refutations; do not ask a text model to invent lines at runtime. Distinguish “mate in N” from “find the tactic” in instructions, scorekeeping and share cards.
16. Monetisation and performance acceptance criteria
- Board, legal move handling, hints and printing work when all ad scripts are blocked.
- No ad overlaps the board or moves controls on standard phone widths.
- No ad click or new window is caused by a puzzle move, hint, reset, show solution, share or print action.
- Consent decisions are honoured by network activity, not merely stored by a UI.
- Cookie inventory is checked for first load, accepted, rejected and withdrawn states in supported regions.
- Ad scripts are loaded only with approved configuration and are covered by regression checks.
- Compare Core Web Vitals and solve completion before/after enabling ads; roll back formats that cause substantial UX degradation or complaints.
- No incentivised, purchased bot, click-exchange or self-generated traffic.
- Clearly mark advertisements as ads where the creative or placement could otherwise be confused with Chessbitz content.
17. Release gates
Gate A - identity and rights
- Trademark/domain/social clearance documented; decision on name and logo recorded.
- Dataset licence snapshot and exact source export archived.
- Package and asset SBOM complete; all licenses approved; THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md published.
- All puzzle content has source and validation metadata; takedown/denylist path tested.
Gate B - privacy and advertising
- Legal operator identity, address and working contact filled in every policy.
- Real production cookie/storage scan completed and cookie table reflects observed behaviour.
- CMP/ad-tag compatibility tested; no required non-essential tracking loads before consent where consent is required.
- Monetag publisher agreement, accepted formats, site approval and child-audience handling reviewed.
- Privacy, cookies, terms, disclaimer, licensing, copyright, security and accessibility pages published and linked.
Gate C - security
- MFA and least-privilege CI configured; no secrets in public assets.
- HTTPS/security headers verified; CSP tested with actual ads.
- Dependency/secret/license scans pass or have documented, accepted exceptions.
- Incident and rollback contacts/runbooks tested; CERT-In scope/log questions answered.
Gate D - product and SEO
- Puzzle replay/data tests pass; mobile, keyboard and screen-reader QA performed.
- PDF output checked for A4/Letter page breaks and no answer leakage on question-only exports.
- Stable URLs, canonical tags, sitemap and robots rules tested.
- No thin mass-generated pages or broken social-preview URLs.
No launch should be declared “compliant” merely because documents exist. Gate B must reflect the running production code and actual advertising vendors.
